ms":{"symbol":"NYSE:XOM","text":"NYSE:XOM"}}," is due to report its Q3 2026 results before the market opens on Friday, with investors assessing whether stronger refining margins can offset weaker upstream earnings amid volatile oil prices. The stock has climbed to record highs, supported by a refining windfall linked to Middle East supply disruptions."]},{"type":"p","children":["In Q2, adjusted EPS of $3.52 fell short of consensus, while revenue exceeded $114B and topped expectations. The mixed results set the stage for a quarter in which refining gains and lower upstream realizations are expected to produce sharply divergent contributions across the business."]},{"type":"p","children":["In a recent filing, Exxon said higher refining margins could add $4.5B to Q3 Energy Products earnings compared with Q2, while Specialty Products could gain $500M. Chemical Products earnings could decline by $600M due to margin changes, and lower liquids prices could reduce Upstream earnings by $1.3B. Exxon cautioned that the figures were not an earnings estimate. Shares closed at a record $178.98 amid heightened tensions around the Strait of Hormuz and elevated Brent crude prices before oil eased as Washington moved to ease restrictions on Russian oil flows."]},{"type":"p","children":["Analyst views remain divided: Wells Fargo downgraded the stock to Equal Weight, while Morgan Stanley, Goldman Sachs, and Barclays have issued price targets ranging from $169 to $200. Investors will focus on the scale of refining gains, upstream resilience, and management’s outlook for oil-market volatility."]},{"type":"p","children":["Wall Street rates the stock a Buy, while Seeking Alpha's Quant rating holds a more cautious Hold."]},{"type":"list","children":[{"type":"*","children":[{"type":"b","children":["Consensus EPS Estimate:"]}," $4.03"]},{"type":"*","children":[{"type":"b","children":["Consensus Revenue Estimate:"]}," $106.02B"]},{"type":"*","children":[{"type":"b","children":["Earnings Insigh